{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-548",
              "name": "Directory Listings Enabled on Static File Server",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Directory Listings Enabled on Static File Server"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Python's built-in `http.server` has directory listing enabled on multiple paths (`/vendor/`, `/vendor/three/`, `/vendor/gsap/`, `/vendor/lenis/`, `/js/`, `/docs/`), exposing the full file tree structure and exact library versions to unauthenticated remote attackers."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Python's built-in `http.server` has directory listing enabled on multiple paths (`/vendor/`, `/vendor/three/`, `/vendor/gsap/`, `/vendor/lenis/`, `/js/`, `/docs/`), exposing the full file tree structure and exact library versions to unauthenticated remote attackers.\n\nAttackers can enumerate all static assets, discover library versions (Three.js r170, GSAP 3.12.5, Lenis), and map the complete application structure. This facilitates targeted attacks against known vulnerabilities in specific library versions and provides reconnaissance for further attacks.\n\nDisable directory listing by either: (1) Adding an index.html file to each directory, (2) Running the server with a custom handler that overrides `list_directory` to return 403/404, or (3) Using a production web server (nginx, Apache) with directory indexing disabled. For Python http.server, subclass `SimpleHTTPRequestHandler` and override `list_directory` to return an error response.",
                "markdown": "Python's built-in `http.server` has directory listing enabled on multiple paths (`/vendor/`, `/vendor/three/`, `/vendor/gsap/`, `/vendor/lenis/`, `/js/`, `/docs/`), exposing the full file tree structure and exact library versions to unauthenticated remote attackers.\n\nAttackers can enumerate all static assets, discover library versions (Three.js r170, GSAP 3.12.5, Lenis), and map the complete application structure. This facilitates targeted attacks against known vulnerabilities in specific library versions and provides reconnaissance for further attacks.\n\nDisable directory listing by either: (1) Adding an index.html file to each directory, (2) Running the server with a custom handler that overrides `list_directory` to return 403/404, or (3) Using a production web server (nginx, Apache) with directory indexing disabled. For Python http.server, subclass `SimpleHTTPRequestHandler` and override `list_directory` to return an error response."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-548",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/548.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-693",
              "name": "Missing Security Headers on Static File Server",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing Security Headers on Static File Server"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Python `http.server` does not set any security headers, leaving the application exposed to various client-side attacks and information disclosure."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Python `http.server` does not set any security headers, leaving the application exposed to various client-side attacks and information disclosure.\n\nAbsence of security headers increases risk of clickjacking (no X-Frame-Options), MIME type sniffing attacks (no X-Content-Type-Options), reduces defense-in-depth against XSS (no CSP), and leaks server technology stack (Server header). The Server header reveals Python 3.12.10 which could aid version-specific attacks.\n\nImplement security headers by: (1) Subclassing `SimpleHTTPRequestHandler` and overriding `end_headers` to add security headers, (2) Deploying behind a reverse proxy (nginx, Apache, Cloudflare) that adds security headers, or (3) Using a production web server instead of Python's development server. Minimum recommended headers: Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options: DENY, X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin.",
                "markdown": "The Python `http.server` does not set any security headers, leaving the application exposed to various client-side attacks and information disclosure.\n\nAbsence of security headers increases risk of clickjacking (no X-Frame-Options), MIME type sniffing attacks (no X-Content-Type-Options), reduces defense-in-depth against XSS (no CSP), and leaks server technology stack (Server header). The Server header reveals Python 3.12.10 which could aid version-specific attacks.\n\nImplement security headers by: (1) Subclassing `SimpleHTTPRequestHandler` and overriding `end_headers` to add security headers, (2) Deploying behind a reverse proxy (nginx, Apache, Cloudflare) that adds security headers, or (3) Using a production web server instead of Python's development server. Minimum recommended headers: Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options: DENY, X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-693",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/693.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "strix-coverage/general-attack-surface-mapping-and-technology-identification",
              "name": "General attack surface mapping and technology identification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Coverage: General attack surface mapping and technology identification"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Coverage of General attack surface mapping and technology identification across the assessed attack surface."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Coverage of General attack surface mapping and technology identification across the assessed attack surface.",
                "markdown": "Coverage of General attack surface mapping and technology identification across the assessed attack surface."
